window.docContents[70].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Compactified-radial-modes":{"contents":"A decaying radial profile can be written in a bounded angular variable as\n. Factoring out the cosine turns the\nweighted derivative into a simple operator on .\n\nDefineAt each point where  is differentiable,For either  or ,\nwith any real frequency ,The identity extends termwise to any finite expansion with real coefficients\nand frequencies. It gives an exact transformation of the radial coefficients;\nthe finite index set, its coefficients, and its frequencies are arbitrary. A spherical\ndivergence-free reconstruction also needs the angular basis and the physical\ncoordinate change, which are separate from this one-variable identity.\n\nDifferentiate  by the product rule and use\n to obtain the weighted-derivative identity.\nCAS checks the derivatives of the sine and cosine modes. Double-angle and\naddition formulas then give the two neighboring frequencies. Finite-sum\ndifferentiation and linearity yield the expansion formula.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.3. Compactified radial modes","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Compactified-radial-modes"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Classical-solutions":{"contents":"A finite-time pointwise solution consists of velocity and pressure on\n, the momentum equation, incompressibility, and the\nprescribed value at . The equation is imposed on the open positive-time\ndomain using totalized ambient derivatives, so a residual solution carries\nno differentiability.\n\nSmoothness at positive times turns the totalized derivatives into classical\nones.\n\nA finite-time smooth solution adds ambient smoothness at positive times and\none-sided continuity at the initial surface. A global pointwise residual\nsolution is posed on ; it restricts to every\nfinite positive horizon. Global smoothness is relative to the physical closed half-space\n, so nothing is assumed at negative times.\nRestricting a global smooth solution to a positive finite horizon gives a\nfinite-time smooth solution, smoothness included. As for Euler, a smooth\nsolution is a weak solution only once the  bounds, weak-gradient\nintegrability, decay, and spacetime integrability of the weak class are proved.\n\nWith classical derivatives available, the divergence of the momentum equation\ncan be taken as for Euler; the viscous term is the only new contribution.\n\nAt every positive-time point of a smooth Navier–Stokes solution,The viscosity does not alter this pressure source. For a componentwise\n incompressible velocity,The result is available for both finite-time and global smooth solutions.\n\nTake the spatial divergence of momentum. Local incompressibility makes the\ndivergence of  vanish. Commutation of the spatial divergence\nwith the vector Laplacian makes the viscous contribution vanish as well.\nThe remaining identity is exactly the pressure Poisson residual equation.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009Stokes and Navier–Stokes Flow\u0009Stokes and Incompressible Navier–Stokes","header":"8.5.1.3. Classical solutions","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--Stokes-and-Navier___Stokes-Flow--Stokes-and-Incompressible-Navier___Stokes--Classical-solutions"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Compactness-from-space-and-time-regularity":{"contents":"Spatial compactness alone leaves an obvious escape route: oscillation in\ntime. Fix  and put\n on  for positive integers . These\ntrajectories are uniformly bounded in , yet for ,\n\n\n\nThey have no strongly convergent subsequence in that space. Their time\nderivatives grow like  in , which is exactly the\nbehavior the second Aubin–Lions bound excludes.\n\nThe Aubin–Lions argument has a spatial half and a temporal half. In the\ntemporal half, Lions–Magenes regularity of the time derivative gives\nevery finite-dimensional observable of a bounded family a common Hölder\nmodulus of order one half, and Arzelà–Ascoli then makes those observables\nprecompact.\n\nLet  be a Gelfand triple with inclusion , let\n, and let  be a family with  and\n, each equipped with a Lions–Magenes continuous pivot\nrepresentative  whose integrated dual derivative is\n. For a fixed test vector  and all ,If  for all  and , and\n for all ,\nthen the scalar coefficient curves , regarded\nas bounded continuous functions on , have compact closure in the\nuniform norm.For a finite-dimensional trial space  with a stable trial\nprojection , the projected curves satisfy the analogous moduluswhere  is the pivot-mass-inverted restriction of the dual\nderivative to , and under uniform bounds  and  on the two\nquantities the family  has compact closure in\n. The underlying abstract step is that a uniformly bounded family\nof continuous curves into a proper normed space with a common modulus\n is relatively compact in the\nuniform norm.\n\nThe integrated dual derivative gives the pairing identity\n, and\nCauchy–Schwarz in time bounds the interval integral of an  function\nby  times its  norm. For the projection, the increment\n is the Bochner integral of the mass-inverted\nrestricted dual derivative, and the same estimate applies. The pivot bound\nand Cauchy–Schwarz bound the curves uniformly, so they take values in a\ncompact ball of the finite-dimensional target; the common modulus gives\nequicontinuity, and Arzelà–Ascoli yields compact closure.\n\nNow combine the two halves. Compactness of  supplies\nfinite-dimensional projections  with\n\n\n\nAfter integrating in time, the discarded spatial tail is uniformly small\nfor the whole bounded family. Fix  first: the time-derivative bound\ncontrols the finitely many retained coefficient curves, so temporal\ncompactness makes those curves precompact. Choose a finite approximation for\nthem and then add back the small tail. This order matters: the temporal\nconstants may depend on , while the final conclusion is strong\ncompactness in ; strong compactness in  does not follow.\n\nLet  be real Hilbert spaces with a Gelfand triple\n whose inclusion  is compact.\nEquip each trajectory  and its dual time derivative \nwith a Lions–Magenes continuous pivot evolution.\nSupposeon a finite interval  with . Then the family  has compact\nclosure in .\n\nThe compact inclusion produces canonical finite-dimensional spectral trial\nspaces. Their pivot projections converge uniformly on the compact image of\nthe energy unit ball, hence their Bochner lifts make the spatial tails\nuniformly small on every -bounded family.At one fixed trial dimension, the Lions–Magenes pairing identity expresses\nthe projected trajectory increment as the interval integral of the\nmass-inverted restricted dual derivative. The derivative bound gives a\ncommon one-half Hölder modulus, while the quantitative time-Sobolev estimate\ngives a common pivot bound. Finite-dimensional Arzelà–Ascoli therefore makes\nthe projected family precompact. A finite-net argument combines that compact\nprojected family with the uniform spatial tail and yields compact closure in\n.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Weak Evolution\u0009Weak Evolution and Galerkin Approximation\u0009Aubin–Lions Compactness","header":"6.3.1.10.1. Compactness from space and time regularity","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Weak-Evolution--Weak-Evolution-and-Galerkin-Approximation--Aubin___Lions-Compactness--Compactness-from-space-and-time-regularity"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--FloatLib___-exact-algebra___-and-CEGA":{"contents":"FloatLib is our library for connecting executable floating-point arithmetic to\nexact specifications and theorems about decoded real values\n. Its\nofficial guide\ndevelops this connection through arithmetic and interval examples. TorchLean\nalso uses FloatLib , applying the arithmetic\nlibrary in a different mathematical development.\n\nIn PDEAtlas, exact symbolic computation supplies identities, derivatives, and\nanalytic side conditions. CEGA's ordinary interval queries use rational\nendpoints and exact rational calculations to certify bounds on real functions.\nFloatLib supplies their interval arithmetic, elementary-function enclosures,\nand outward rounding. PDEAtlas's rational interval adds an endpoint-order proof\nto FloatLib.Numerics.Interval ℚ. For generic expression evaluation, a\nsemantic translation maps CAS syntax into FloatLib's own expression language;\nupstream backends supply the endpoint representation and numerical operations.\nTheir containment theorems cover arbitrary real members of the input intervals.\nFailed decoding, unsupported domains, or unrepresentable output bounds return\nno enclosure.\n\nFloatLib also provides certified IEEE binary32 operations; CEGA's concrete\nbackend is rational and does not dispatch to them.\n\nThe connection to the CAS is explicit in the derivative-enclosure bridge.\nPDEAtlas's CompiledDerivative certificate supplies the derivative expression\nand its analytic justification on the chosen panel. The numerical evaluator\nmust enclose that same expression, with an input interval covering the whole\npanel. Its soundness theorem then gives a bound on the requested derivative.\nMore generally, the shared real-interval interface allows a proved enclosure\nto produce CEGA enclosure and residual certificates. The bridge requires a\nproof that the original real function lies in the interval throughout the\nstated domain.\n\nFloatLib proves an exponential enclosure using argument reduction and a Taylor remainder,\nthen rounds its bounds outward when the chosen representation requires it.\nPDEAtlas connects that containment theorem to the original symbolic expression\nand its domain. Derivative and quadrature certificates add the analytic\nregularity and remainder estimates needed for the surrounding PDE argument.\n\nThe numerical APIs also contain scalar and piecewise Taylor approximation\ncertificates, whose general constructors take real Taylor coefficients and so\nare noncomputable; the derivative enclosures and rational error calculations\nthat feed them are executable. The polynomial subsystem includes a global root\nisolator built from checked square-free reduction, a generated Cauchy bound,\nand Sturm subdivision. When its finite search succeeds, the resulting atlas\ncovers every distinct real root.\n\nArmstrong and collaborators describe extensive model assistance under human\nsupervision ; Tau Ceti organizes assisted\nformalization around a shared library .\nPDEAtlas aims to make its Sobolev, weak-solution, and certified-estimate\ninfrastructure reusable by other Mathlib-based PDE projects, and to prepare\nsuitable components for review in Mathlib and Tau Ceti.\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.6. FloatLib, exact algebra, and CEGA","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--FloatLib___-exact-algebra___-and-CEGA"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem--Example___-the-Dirichlet-energy":{"contents":"The prototypical application of the direct method is the Dirichlet energy\n\n\n\nwhere , , is a bounded open set and\n. This energy governs the Poisson equation\n with Dirichlet boundary conditions. Its load term \ndoes not in general satisfy the pointwise lower bound uniform in  used\nby the extended mixed-energy theorem. Here Poincaré controls that term after\nintegration. Let us trace the direct-method argument on  through its\nfour steps.\n\nStep 1: Coercivity. The Poincaré inequality on  gives a\nconstant  with . Together\nwith Cauchy–Schwarz and Young's inequality,\n\n\n\nThus  as , confirming\ncoercivity.\n\nStep 2: Bounded minimizing sequence. Let  be a minimizing\nsequence: . By coercivity,  is\nbounded. The Poincaré inequality then bounds .\n\nStep 3: Weak convergence and compactness. Since  is a\nreflexive Hilbert space, the Eberlein–Šmulian theorem extracts a subsequence\n weakly in . The gradient map is\nbounded and linear, so the gradients converge weakly in  as well.\nThese weak limits suffice for the two terms of this energy.\n\nStep 4: Lower semicontinuity. The gradient term is convex and continuous\nin the strong  norm, hence weakly lower semicontinuous:\n\n\n\nFor the load term, Cauchy–Schwarz and Poincaré give\n.\nThus  is a bounded linear functional on\n and is continuous for weak convergence. Therefore\n\n\n\nSince  is admissible, , and  is a minimizer.\n\nUniqueness. The energy  is strictly convex (the Hessian in direction\n is  for ), so the minimizer is unique.\n\nEuler–Lagrange equation. Stationarity  for all\n gives\n\n\n\nwhich is the weak form of  with  on .\nIn the library, the zero-boundary Sobolev space, Dirichlet form, and load\nfunctional of this example come from the\nPoisson Dirichlet energy rather than\nfrom the integral-functional minimizer theorem.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method\u0009Sobolev Integral Functionals\u0009Scope of the integral theorem","header":"9.3.1.7.2.1. Example: the Dirichlet energy","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Sobolev-Integral-Functionals--Scope-of-the-integral-theorem--Example___-the-Dirichlet-energy"}});